{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788305736,"nonce":"sGCZ1E2/J2k1wocTxzYVKydlGZk=","message":"Implemented on `spec/wake-halts`, merged up to `main` at `a2bf74f` (so `1113019` is in). Build clean, `-c Release`, 0 warnings; **no assembly on the branch** — `git checkout -- Assemblies/` after every build. **Nothing here has met a bench.**\n\n## What I checked before believing the issue, and one correction\n\nGrepped rather than trusted, as the brief asks.\n\n**The scope's table is wrong about `thermal`.** `grep -n 'Halt(' Source/AutoRimmer/TimeDriver.cs` returns eleven call sites and **none of them names `thermal`**. The governor only steps the SPEED down one notch (`OneNotchDown`, reported as `speedChanges[].by == \"thermal\"`); it never stops an advance. The fourth unconditional halt is `red_error`'s own path in `NoticeRedError`, which is separate from `Notice` and sits upstream of the journal's dedupe cap. Nothing turns on this — four unconditional halts is still four — but the table should not be quoted as source.\n\n**Everything else in the measurement holds.** `Halt(\"letter\"…)`, `Halt(\"threat\"…)`, `Halt(\"alert\"…)` and `Halt(\"event\"…)` were all inside `switch (until)`; `dialog` and `casualty` return before it.\n\n**The orchestrator's `CheckUntilKeys` finding holds and is worse than \"opt-in\".** `TimeDriver.cs`, `CheckUntilKeys`: `if (found.Count \u003e 1) throw … \"until takes ONE matcher and was given \" + found.Count`. So the halts were **mutually exclusive** — an agent already waiting on `until:{condition:{…}}` could not *also* ask to be woken by a raid, and there was no workaround available even to an agent that knew the hazard and wanted to guard against it. That is now the sharpest check in the suite (phase 3.4): the two-matcher refusal must still stand, **and** a letter must wake a condition-armed advance anyway.\n\n## Scope, item by item\n\n**1 — the letter halt is unconditional.** Every letter, no filter, no allow-list.\n\n**2 — the alert halt is unconditional** on the `alert_on` transition, subject to the mute.\n\n**THE MATCHERS ARE EVALUATED FIRST, and that is the whole answer to \"the two must not collide.\"** Both halts fire on the same journal row; what differs is the token the caller gets back, and the caller's name has to win. An advance armed `until:{threat}` that stopped on a `ThreatBig` letter must report `reason:\"threat\"` — running the wake first would have renamed every explicit wait to `\"letter\"` and broken a matcher shipped since 1.3 **invisibly**, since the advance still stops at the same tick on the same event. Each matcher arm now `return`s, so the wake below sees only rows the caller did not ask about.\n\n`halted_on.armed_by` is `\"until\"` or `\"default\"` and is **present on both**, never inferred from an absence. That is the field that makes \"still works as an explicit wait\" assertable.\n\n`until:{event}` is deliberately **not** stamped with `kind`/`armed_by`: it matches an arbitrary journal type and `downed`/`death` payloads own `kind` themselves (`colonist|slave|animal|mech`), so stamping would rewrite the caller's data. `letter` and `alert_on` have fixed documented key sets (JOURNAL.md) and are safe to stamp.\n\n**3 — `alert-mute`, and the four questions the issue asked me to design and justify.**\n\nShaped like `threat-pardon`, argument for argument, because the two are the same ruling applied to two subjects:\n\n    alert-mute {}                                   -\u003e the set + the live candidates\n    alert-mute {ids:[\"Alert_LowFood\"], reason:\"…\"}   -\u003e mute (reason REQUIRED)\n    alert-mute {ids:[…], release:true}               -\u003e un-mute\n    alert-mute {release_all:true}                    -\u003e un-mute everything\n\n* **Name.** `alert-mute` — subject-then-action, matching `threat-pardon`. An agent that has learned one should not have to learn the other.\n* **Does it have a game-side gate? NO, and I checked rather than assumed.** `RimWorld/AlertsReadout.cs` has no mute, no dismiss and no hide — its only per-alert interaction is `Alert.OnClick`'s jump-to-target — and `Verse/Prefs.cs` carries no alert preference at all. RimWorld has no concept of an alert you have stopped caring about, because a human just looks past one. Citing a member would dress our invention as the game's, which is the failure DESIGN §Action model exists to prevent, so `threat-pardon`'s disposition is copied: the precondition is stated as OURS and made narrow. An id must name a real `Alert` subclass in the loaded assemblies (`typeof(Alert).AllSubclassesNonAbstract()`) — which covers modded alerts free and, unlike `AlertsReadout.allAlertTypesCached`, does not exclude the `Alert_Custom` subclasses our own fixture alerts are. A typo is **refused** with near-misses rather than stored, because a mute that silently matches nothing is worse than no mute: the agent believes it is covered. Liveness is deliberately **not** required — pre-muting before a long build is the normal case.\n* **Permanent or expiring? PERMANENT until released, and the obvious lapse rule was killed on evidence.** `threat-pardon` lapses because the game reifies \"still asleep\" twice (`LordToil_Sleep`, `CompCanBeDormant.Awake`). The analogue here would be \"un-mute when it returns at a higher priority\" — and `Alert.Priority` is `public virtual AlertPriority Priority =\u003e defaultPriority` with **exactly one declaration in the whole decompiled 1.6 tree**. No vanilla alert overrides it, so priority is a per-class constant, cannot change under a mute, and that rule would never fire once. A timed expiry was rejected for a stronger reason: an expiry the agent did not choose is a wake it did not ask for, at a tick it cannot predict, for a decision it already made — the noise the mute exists to remove, deferred rather than removed.\n* **Muted while already ON?** Nothing now, something later, and it is TOLD rather than left to be inferred. `alert_on` is a transition, so an alert already on has emitted its event and there is no pending wake to cancel; the mute takes effect on the next on-cycle. The applied row carries `already_on` and a `note` saying exactly that.\n\n**Visible in the digest, which was the non-negotiable part.** `digest.alerts` gains `muted` (id, reason, `live`), `muted_count`, `muted_live`, and a `muted` flag on each active row. The standalone list sits **outside the `AlertCap` truncation** on purpose: the cap drops live rows by priority, and a standing decision hidden by a display budget is `[[seek-off-is-a-decision-to-flee]]` with a different subject. Uncapped is safe because the list is bounded by what the AGENT muted, one act at a time with a required reason, not by anything the colony can generate.\n\n`until:{alert:\"X\"}` **overrides a mute**, since the matchers run first: \"wait FOR this\" is a different question from \"wake me for this\", and the one asked *this call* outranks a standing decision from an earlier day.\n\nOne implementation note worth flagging for review: `TimeDriver.Notice` is documented \"any thread\" and may not touch Verse, and `Current.Game.GetComponent` is Verse. So the wake reads a **static volatile mirror** that the component replaces wholesale on every mutation (and republishes from `FinalizeInit`/`LoadedGame`/`StartedNewGame`, so a new colony cannot inherit the old one's mutes). Every write funnels through one `Publish()`, the way `NoteActive` owns the speed high-water mark.\n\n**4 — the escape is `through_news`, a THIRD argument, and the reasoning is not aesthetic.** `through_casualties` says \"my colonists may fall while this runs and I accept that\"; `through_news` says \"do not wake me for things I might act on\". A post-mortem grepping for who accepted casualties must not turn up every run that only wanted to sleep through a trade caravan. They are also asymmetric in **mechanism**: one bypasses an ARM-TIME refusal and shows up in `escaped`/`bypassed`, the other suppresses a DURING-ADVANCE halt whose cost is only knowable at the end. So what it cost is reported separately as `news_rode_past` (count kept whole, first 20 events shown) — an escape that hides the number it bypassed is a silent bypass with a reason string stapled on. What the MUTE swallowed is reported as `muted_alerts`, **unconditionally**, because that is a standing decision doing its work and the agent should be able to watch it rather than infer it from an absence. `through_news` does **not** defeat an explicit `until:{letter}`.\n\n**5 — the dependency on `1113019` is recorded in three places.** A DESIGN entry; the code comment in `TimeDriver.Start` that `1113019` itself left open (\"what ELSE should wake the agent … a day of quiet is only safe to sleep through if the halt set is good enough\") now names `280fb78` as its answer and says neither should be reverted without the other; and the suite's `.md`.\n\n## The acceptance, and the two bullets most likely to be skipped\n\n`accept/280fb78-wake-halts.py` + `.md`. Phase 0 shape contract, `--dry-run`, `--selftest` (offline over `accept/runs/s21-20260901/`), `--phase N`, exit 0/1/2. **107 checks planned; 22/22 green offline.**\n\n* **\"A day-long advance on a quiet colony still completes without halting\" — phase 6.** \"It did not halt\" is unprovable on a colony that is alive, and asserting it would make the suite flaky in the one direction that reads as a spec failure. So \"not spurious\" is given a testable meaning: **every halt must name a journal row that exists, of the matching type, at the seq it published.** The phase advances a full day in halt-bounded segments, reads the journal after each, looks up `halted_seq`, and FAILs on any halt with nothing behind it. It then runs the strict form — with the wake escaped, a day-long advance must complete on its bound — and reports the wake tally, which is a number nobody has yet.\n* **\"`until:{letter}` still works and is distinguishable\" — phase 3**, four ways: the explicit wait still halts with `armed_by:\"until\"`; a `ThreatBig` under `until:{threat}` reports `threat` and **not** `letter`; a letter the explicit wait did not want still wakes with `armed_by:\"default\"`; and `until:{alert:\"X\"}` fires even when X is muted.\n\nPlus phase 3.4 for the orchestrator's added bullet, phase 7 for the four distinct reason tokens `accept/4.2-play-loop.py` keys on, and phase 8 offline.\n\n**Two fixtures had to be added, both disclosed here as undeclared additions, both dev-gated and journalled like every other `journal-selftest` step.**\n\n* `alert-at` — an alert injected from inside a TICK. The shipped `alerts` step **cannot** drive this: `AlertScanner.Tick` runs from `GameComponentUpdate`, i.e. every FRAME *including while paused*, so an alert injected from the command drain is diffed and journalled before the next advance even starts and halts nothing. Armed like `722c951`'s `down-at`, fired from `GameComponentTick`. `alert_critical` selects the second fixture class, which gives the suite two distinct ids in one advance — mute one, leave the other, and the halt that does or does not arrive is the whole proof.\n* `letter_def` on the existing `letter` step, defaulting to the `NeutralEvent` it has always sent. The acceptance is \"a letter of ANY def halts, including NeutralEvent and PositiveEvent\" and a one-def fixture cannot test the rule that says the def does not matter. Refused on an unknown def, because a silently-wrong def would send a `NeutralEvent` while the suite believed it had proved `PositiveEvent`.\n\nBoth argument sets are in `SelftestArgs` — the trap `722c951`'s own comment there records, where a clean merge that added a step without its arguments would refuse every call to that step before any step ran.\n\n## Three other suites were edited, and why\n\nConsequences of the ruling, not defects in it.\n\n* **`accept/fc287ba-until-state.py`** is the file most at risk, and **not for the reason the brief expected** — it does not use `until:{letter}` or `until:{threat}` anywhere (`grep 'letter\\|threat\\|alert'` returns one comment). The exposure is the other way round: phase 4 waits ~3,180 ticks for colonists to build 22 elements and asserts `reason:\"layout\"`, so any letter or alert during that build would come back `reason:\"letter\"` and fail a check about something else. Its single `advance()` wrapper already `setdefault`s the other two escapes; `through_news` joins them.\n* **`accept/722c951-advance-halt.py`** is 96/0 and must not regress. Checks `1.7` (`\"ticks\"`), `3.4` (`\"casualty\"`), `4.4` and `5.3` (`\"condition\"`) are all beatable by a letter. `through_news` goes in `_bound()`, the one blanket escape that file carries, with a header saying why it is the exception rather than a retreat. **Safe for the one place it genuinely wants a letter** — phase 1 arms `until:{letter:true}` and the explicit matcher wins over the wake — and the new suite's phase 5.4 asserts that rather than trusting it. Its own `--selftest` is still 30/30.\n* **`accept/4.2-play-loop.py`** gains `through_news` in its escape list and a new `advance-wakes` check: what woke each advance, by name, plus a WARN for every `alert_on` a standing mute swallowed. **None of them FAILs** — being woken is the system working. Re-advancing straight off a wake is deliberately not a second FAIL: a letter is a journal event, so the next advance is already refused `unread-journal` unless the loop reads or escapes, and double-charging one mistake makes the audit worse. Its selftest gains a fixture carrying all three facts and asserts each SURFACES. Still green.\n\n## Filed, not queued\n\n`a30c807` — `accept/fc287ba-until-state.py --dry-run` crashes in phase 3 with a `TypeError` (`(hour + 1) % 24` on a `None`), so the plan for three of its five phases has never been printable. **Pre-existing**, reproduced against `git show main:accept/fc287ba-until-state.py` at `a2bf74f`. p3, with an acceptance section.\n\n## Nothing BLOCKED.","files":null}]}